dicate that there are unobserved factors influencing the Math Score, leading to spurious relationships. Similarly, if the errors exhibit heteroscedasticity, it can result in inefficient estimates, affecting the reliability of hypothesis tests.\n\nIn summary, the Math Score is a pivotal measure in evaluating student performance in mathematics, serving as a key outcome variable in growth models. Its analysis provides valuable insights into educational effectiveness and student learning trajectories, while adherence to the underlying assumptions of the modeling framework is essential for producing valid and reliable results. By understanding the dynamics of Math Scores, educators and researchers can better address the challenges in mathematics education and work towards improving student outcomes.\",18\r\n3810,GRADE LEVEL,,2\r\n3816,TRANSITION,\"Transition refers to the grade-level transition with new, more difficult measures that students experience, impacting their scores.\",1\r\n3818,SPECIAL EDUCATION STATUS,Special Education Status is a variable included in the model to analyze its effect on student performance.,1\r\n3819,SEX,Sex is a demographic variable considered in the model to evaluate its impact on math scores.,1\r\n3820,ENGLISH LANGUAGE LEARNER STATUS,English Language Learner Status is a variable in the model that examines its influence on student performance.,1\r\n3821,FREE/REDUCED LUNCH STATUS,Free/Reduced Lunch Status is a socioeconomic variable included in the model to assess its effect on math scores.,1\r\n3229,MODEL 3,\"**Model 3**\n\nModel 3 is a statistical model utilized in hierarchical linear modeling (HLM) that incorporates both varying intercepts and varying regression coefficients. This model is particularly significant in educational research, where it is often employed to analyze factors influencing student performance across different contexts and levels of data aggregation.\n\nThe formulation of Model 3 allows for the inclusion of multiple p